- [Stripe Checkout UK pricing](https://stripe.com/gb/checkout): `1.5% + 20p` for standard UK cards.
- [Stripe Billing](https://stripe.com/gb/billing/pricing): `0.7%` of billing volume for recurring billing.
- [Stripe Tax Basic](https://stripe.com/tax/pricing): `0.5%` per taxed transaction for no-code Billing/Checkout flows.

PayPal:
- [PayPal UK fees](https://www.paypal.com/gb/business/paypal-business-fees) list `2.9% + fixed fee` for standard domestic commercial transactions, while other card-processing products have separate pricing.
- [PayPal recurring payments](https://www.paypal.com/uk/business/accept-payments/recurring-payments) are built in, but the overall setup is less clean for a subscription app business.

So for a small UK software company, Stripe is usually the easier default.

The most important thing you must not miss

Your real constraint is not PayPal vs Stripe.
It is mobile platform policy.

For iPhone:
- Apple’s terms say subscription services purchased within the app must use In-App Purchase.
- Apple’s [Small Business Program](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/small-business-program/) can reduce commission to `15%` if you qualify.
- Apple says [90% of submissions are reviewed in under 24 hours](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/), but approval is still not guaranteed.

For Android:
- Google Play says [digital products/content in Android apps use Google Play billing](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing).
- Google’s [service fee page](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/112622?hl=en) says automatically renewing subscriptions are `15%`.
- Google also says reviews can take [a few hours up to 7 days or longer in exceptional cases](https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/9859654?hl=en).

For Microsoft Store:
- If you distribute through Microsoft Store and sell digital items used in the app, Microsoft policy says you must use the [Microsoft Store in-product purchase API](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/store-policy-archive/store-policy-7-13).
- If you distribute your Windows app directly from your own website, you can use Stripe normally.

This means:
A single “just use Stripe/PayPal everywhere” plan is not the best architecture for a normal consumer premium app.

Best architecture for your business

Use this model:

1. Your own auth system
Every user has your internal `user_id`.

2. Your own entitlement record
Your backend decides:
- `regular`
- `premium_active`
- `premium_grace`
- `premium_expired`

3. Payment sources by channel
- Web: Stripe Checkout or Payment Links
- Direct Windows app: Stripe Checkout opened in browser/webview, then login reflects entitlement
- iOS: App Store In-App Purchase
- Android: Google Play Billing

4. Webhooks / server notifications
Your backend updates entitlement on:
- successful purchase,
- renewal,
- failed payment,
- cancellation,
- expiry.

5. App login check
Every app asks your backend: “is this user premium?”
Do not trust the device alone.

Fastest small-team way to do cross-platform sync

Use RevenueCat if you want this done quickly and cleanly.

Why:
- RevenueCat says the same [App User ID can access entitlements purchased on any platform](https://www.revenuecat.com/docs/customers/identifying-customers).
- RevenueCat also supports [Stripe web payments](https://www.revenuecat.com/docs/web/integrations/stripe).
- Their customer data model explicitly tracks entitlements unlocked by App Store, Play Store, or Stripe.
- Their homepage says pricing starts after `$2,500` in monthly tracked revenue ([RevenueCat](https://www.revenuecat.com/)).

For your size and timeline, this is probably the highest-leverage tool in the stack.

What I would do if I had to launch in 1 to 2 days

Best launch path:
- Day 1: launch web checkout with Stripe Checkout or Payment Links.
- Day 1: connect Stripe webhooks to your backend and mark users `premium`.
- Day 1: let the Windows app log in and read premium status from backend.
- Day 2: add Stripe customer portal so users can cancel/update cards.
- Day 2+: integrate mobile store billing and RevenueCat, then submit iOS/Android.

Why this is the best path:
- It gets you selling fastest.
- It avoids getting blocked by mobile app review.
- It gives you the clean entitlement system you actually need.
- It lets you add mobile properly instead of hacking around store rules.

What I would not do

I would reject these options:

- PayPal-only as the main billing system.
Reason: workable, but weaker fit for a login-based subscription app business.

- Custom card form on day 1.
Reason: slower, more compliance surface, unnecessary when hosted checkout exists.

- One payment provider across all platforms.
Reason: iOS/Android policy makes this unrealistic for normal premium digital access.

- Trying to push external web billing inside mobile everywhere from day 1.
Reason: there are exceptions in some regions, but it adds policy complexity and review risk. Not the best small-team launch strategy.

UK tax/compliance angle

This matters more than many founders expect.

HMRC says if a UK business supplies digital services to consumers outside the UK, VAT may be due where the consumer is based, and if a digital service is sold via a third-party platform/marketplace, the platform can be responsible for VAT on that supply ([HMRC guidance](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-vat-rules-if-you-supply-digital-services-to-private-consumers)).

Implication:
- App stores reduce a lot of tax/payment burden for mobile.
- Direct Stripe web sales give you more control and better economics, but you own more tax/compliance unless you add Stripe Tax or use a merchant-of-record.

If your customers will be mostly UK first:
- Stripe Billing is fine.

If you want to sell globally from day 1 and do not want tax admin:
- Consider a merchant-of-record like Paddle, or Stripe’s [Managed Payments](https://stripe.com/en-li/pricing), instead of plain Stripe for web sales.

Final recommendation

Best overall setup for you:
- Main direct billing: Stripe
- Mobile billing: Apple + Google
- Entitlement sync: your backend, ideally with RevenueCat
- PayPal: optional later, not first
- Launch order: web/direct Windows first, mobile second

If you force me to answer only “PayPal or Stripe?”:
Stripe.

ASSUMPTIONS

- I assumed your mobile app is a normal consumer app with premium digital features, not a pure “reader app” exception.
- I assumed your Windows app can be distributed directly from your site, not only through Microsoft Store.
- I assumed you want recurring subscriptions or at least account-based paid access, not a one-time lifetime unlock only.
- I assumed speed to launch is more important than adding every payment method on day 1.
- I assumed you want a production-safe setup, not a workaround that risks App Store / Play rejection.

