## Summary
The researcher got the broad direction mostly right: use a backend-owned entitlement model, do not rely on PayPal-only for a subscription app, and do not assume mobile can use the same billing path as web. But the answer has at least one material factual error, an imprecise fee comparison, and missing security/edge-case guidance that makes the proposed implementation risky.

Checked against current docs from [Microsoft Store](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/publish/store-policy-archive/store-policy-7-16-1), [Stripe Checkout](https://stripe.com/gb/payments/checkout), [Stripe Billing](https://stripe.com/gb/billing/pricing), [PayPal UK fees](https://www.paypal.com/gb/business/paypal-business-fees), [Google Play billing/security](https://developer.android.com/google/play/billing/security), [Apple enrollment](https://developer.apple.com/help/account/membership/program-enrollment/), [HMRC VAT](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-vat-rules-if-you-supply-digital-services-to-private-consumers), [Paddle pricing](https://www.paddle.com/pricing), and [Stripe Managed Payments](https://stripe.com/managed-payments).

## Issues Found
- [HIGH] Microsoft Store guidance is factually wrong
- Location: “For Microsoft Store” under “The most important thing you must not miss”
- Problem: The answer says Microsoft Store distribution requires Microsoft’s in-product purchase API for digital items. Current Microsoft Store policy 7.16.1 says non-game PC products may use either a secure third-party purchase API or Microsoft’s API. That error can push the user into an unnecessary architecture split.
- Fix: Replace that section with: “If your Windows app is a non-game PC app, Microsoft Store currently allows a secure third-party purchase API such as Stripe/Paddle/PayPal, subject to Store policy requirements and correct product declarations. Direct website distribution also allows Stripe normally.”

- [HIGH] The pricing section is not apples-to-apples
- Location: “Pricing direction”
- Problem: The answer compares Stripe’s card fee plus Billing fee plus Tax fee against PayPal’s general domestic commercial fee, which is not a like-for-like subscription/web checkout comparison. PayPal’s own UK fee page lists multiple products and rates, including `1.2% + fixed fee` for some card-processing/payment-link flows, while Stripe Billing adds `0.7%` on top of payment fees and Stripe Tax adds `0.5%` only if used. The conclusion may still be “Stripe is easier,” but the fee math shown does not prove it.
- Fix: Rewrite as a scenario table, for example:
  - Stripe web subscription: `1.5% + 20p` payments + `0.7%` Billing + optional `0.5%` Tax
  - PayPal wallet checkout: `2.9% + fixed fee`
  - PayPal card-focused checkout/payment links: see current product-specific rate page
  - Merchant of record: higher fee, but tax/compliance included

- [HIGH] The entitlement sync design is insecure as written
- Location: “4. Webhooks / server notifications” and “5. App login check”
- Problem: The answer tells the user to update premium status from payment events but omits signature verification, idempotency, replay protection, and provider re-checks. Stripe explicitly warns that without webhook verification, attackers can grant account access. Google explicitly requires server-side purchase verification before granting entitlements.
- Fix: Add a minimum-safe server flow:
```ts
app.post('/webhooks/stripe', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), async (req, res) => {
  let event;
  try {
    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(
      req.body,
      req.headers['stripe-signature']!,
      process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!
    );
  } catch {
    return res.status(400).send('bad signature');
  }

  if (await db.webhookEvent.findUnique({ where: { providerEventId: event.id } })) {
    return res.sendStatus(200);
  }

  await db.$transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.webhookEvent.create({ data: { providerEventId: event.id, provider: 'stripe' } });
    if (event.type.startsWith('customer.subscription.')) {
      const sub = event.data.object as Stripe.Subscription;
      await syncStripeEntitlement(tx, sub);
    }
  });

  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```
